    <statement><p>Consider the graph of the function <m>f(x)</m> shown below.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap2Sec2__Q15_pg-image-1.png" width="41%"/>
<p>Using this graph, for each of the following pairs of numbers
decide which is larger.  <em>Be sure that you can explain your
answer.</em></p>
<p><alert>A.</alert>
<m>f(12)</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0001"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>&lt;</p>
</li><li>
<p>=</p>
</li><li>
<p>&gt;</p>
</li></ul> <m>f(15)</m></p>
<p><alert>B.</alert>
<m>f(12) - f(9)</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0002"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>&lt;</p>
</li><li>
<p>=</p>
</li><li>
<p>&gt;</p>
</li></ul> <m>f(9) - f(6)</m></p>
<p><alert>C.</alert>
<m>\frac{f(9) - f(6)}{9 - 6}</m>
  <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0003"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>&lt;</p>
</li><li>
<p>=</p>
</li><li>
<p>&gt;</p>
</li></ul>
  <m>\frac{f(12) - f(6)}{12 - 6}</m></p>
<p><alert>D.</alert>
<m>f'(6)</m> <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0004"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>&lt;</p>
</li><li>
<p>=</p>
</li><li>
<p>&gt;</p>
</li></ul> <m>f'(15)</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\verb!&lt;!}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\verb!&gt;!}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\verb!&lt;!}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>{\verb!&lt;!}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert>A.</alert>
Since <m>f</m> is increasing,
<m>f(12)</m> &lt; <m>f(15)</m>.



<alert>B.</alert>
From the figure, it appears that
<m>f(12) - f(9)</m> &gt;
<m>f(9) - f(6)</m>.



<alert>C.</alert>
The quantity <m>\frac{f(9)-f(6)}{9-6}</m>
represents the slope of the secant line connecting the points on the graph
at <m>x=6</m> and <m>x=9</m>. This is less than the slope of the
secant line connecting the points at <m>x=6</m> and <m>x=12</m>, which
is <m>\frac{f(12)-f(6)}{12-6}</m>



<alert>D.</alert>
The function is less steep at <m>x=6</m> than at <m>x=15</m>, so
<m>f'(6)</m> &lt; <m>f'(15)</m>.



</solution>
